# Overview

The other vignettes each explain one part of `bayesnec`: what the model set is,
what a `bayesmanecfit` is, how priors are built, how to handle a two-block
response. This one runs a single analysis from data to reportable estimate, in
the order the steps actually occur, and shows the two that the others state but
do not demonstrate: **choosing the family from the data**, and **removing a
model from the set and seeing what that does to the answer**.

The workflow is:

1. Prepare the data and choose the family.
2. Fit the candidate set.
3. Check the sampler.
4. Check the fit.
5. Exclude what fails, and re-inspect the weights.
6. Report.

Steps 3 and 5 are the ones with consequences. `vignette("example2")` notes that
there is little harm in leaving a poorly *shaped* model in the set with low
weight, which is true, but a model that has not **converged** is a different
matter. Whether removing it changes the reported estimate cannot be known before
the screen is run: it depends on how much weight the failing models carry, and
on whether those models disagree with the ones retained. Those are two separate
questions, and the worked examples below give three different answers to them.

```{r libraries}
library(bayesnec)
library(ggplot2)
```

# 1. Data preparation and family choice

The `nassarius` data are survival counts for a marine gastropod exposed to a
contaminant, recorded per individual within replicate tanks.

```{r data}
data(nassarius)
head(nassarius)
```

Survival is binary per individual, so the natural unit for a
concentration-response model is the tank: the number alive out of the number
exposed.

```{r aggregate}
surv <- aggregate(alive ~ dose + tank + contaminant,
                  data = nassarius, FUN = function(x) c(suc = sum(x),
                                                        tot = length(x)))
surv <- do.call(data.frame, surv)
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.suc"] <- "suc"
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.tot"] <- "tot"
surv <- surv[surv$contaminant == "A", ]
head(surv)
```

## The pre-fit screen

The obvious family for successes-out-of-trials is `binomial`. A binomial model
fixes the variance at $np(1-p)$, and replicate tanks often vary by more than
that, because animals within a tank share conditions and the effective sample
size is therefore smaller than the number of individuals. Fitting the whole
candidate set under a family that cannot represent the spread commits every
model in it to an assumption the data do not support.

The screen requires one model rather than the whole set.

```{r screen}
screen <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "nec4param"),
               data = surv, family = binomial(link = "identity"),
               iter = 2e3, chains = 2, seed = 17)
dispersion(screen, summary = TRUE)
```

`dispersion()` returns, for each posterior draw, the ratio of the observed to
the simulated sum of squared Pearson residuals. Its null value is exactly 1 by
construction: a family whose variance assumption holds simulates residuals of
the same magnitude as those observed. A full posterior is returned, so the
decision is read from the **interval**, which states how strong the evidence of
a departure from 1 is, rather than from the point estimate, which does not.

```{r family-choice}
disp <- dispersion(screen, summary = TRUE)
fam <- if (disp[["Q2.5"]] > 1) "beta_binomial" else "binomial"
fam
```

The interval runs from `r round(disp[["Q2.5"]], 2)` to
`r round(disp[["Q97.5"]], 2)`. It includes 1, so there is no evidence against
the binomial variance assumption and the analysis proceeds under `binomial`.
Had the lower bound cleared 1, `beta_binomial` would be the choice. The point
estimate is `r round(disp[["Estimate"]], 2)`. Judging that number against a
fixed cutoff would decide the case on a quantity whose uncertainty the screen
has just measured, and `r nrow(surv)` tanks across
`r length(unique(surv$dose))` doses do not determine the ratio precisely.
`vignette("example1")` applies the same convention, describing values above 1 as
over-dispersion and below 1 as under-dispersion, with no threshold.

`dispersion()` returns a named vector --- `Estimate`, `Q2.5`, `Q97.5` --- and
only for `poisson` and `binomial`, the two families whose variance is fixed by
the mean. For anything else it returns an empty vector, which is the correct
answer rather than an error: there is no over-dispersion decision to make when
the family already carries a free dispersion parameter.

`bnec()` forces `link = "identity"` on every family so that `top`, `bot` and
`nec` stay on the response scale --- see `vignette("example1")`.

# 2. Fitting the candidate set

```{r fit, results = "hide"}
fit <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "decline"),
            data = surv, family = fam,
            iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

Two arguments matter once a fit is marginal:

- `iter` and `warmup`. The defaults are generous, but a model with a poorly
  identified `nec` may need more. Raising `iter` is the first thing to try when
  Rhat is marginal.
- `control = list(adapt_delta = 0.99, max_treedepth = 12)`. Raise `adapt_delta`
  when there are divergent transitions; raise `max_treedepth` when the sampler
  reports hitting it. Both cost time and neither repairs a misspecified model.

## The set as fitted

`model = "decline"` requests `r length(models()$decline)` equations, and fewer
than that are averaged over. Two things reduce the set, and they are different:
some equations are excluded before fitting because they are invalid for the
family, and others are attempted and fail.

```{r set-fitted}
requested <- models()$decline
failed <- names(failed_models(fit))
excluded <- setdiff(requested, c(fit$success_models, failed))
list(fitted = fit$success_models, failed = failed, excluded = excluded)
```

`neclin` and `ecxlin` decay by subtraction and can therefore predict a negative
mean, which a zero-bounded family cannot represent. `bnec()` drops them and
issues a message at fit time. Models that fail to fit are recorded rather than
silently discarded, and can be re-tried individually with `amend(add = )`:

```{r failed}
failed_models(fit)
```

The three categories account for every requested equation, which is what a
methods section has to state. Neither the diagnostic table in step 3 nor
`failed_models()` alone recovers the equations that were never attempted.

# 3. Sampler diagnostics

The first question is whether the sampler worked, not whether the model is any
good, which is step 4.

`check_sampling()` reports per candidate model, so the whole set is screened in
one call rather than model by model.

```{r sampling}
check_sampling(fit)
```

One row per fitted model, with the three diagnostics that decide whether a fit
is usable at all: the largest Rhat, the smallest effective sample size, and the
number of divergent transitions.

Read `min_ess` rather than `min_ess_ratio` when deciding. The default cutoff of
400 is the recommendation that both bulk and tail effective sample size exceed
100 per chain [@Vehtari2021], evaluated at the four chains `bnec()` fits by
default. The fits in this vignette use three chains to keep it quick, so 400 is
a stricter target here than the recommendation requires; 300 would be the
matching value, and `ess_cutoff` is an argument. The ratio is reported beside
the count so that a model that passed because 8000 draws were taken can be told
apart from one that passed efficiently.

All three thresholds are arguments. The Rhat default of 1.01 and the ESS default
of 400 follow published recommendations; **the divergence default of 10 does
not**. Stan's own guidance is that *any* divergence means the sampler failed to
explore the posterior. Ten is pragmatic, from practice with these non-linear
models, which routinely produce a handful near a boundary. State whichever
values you use, because they will be quoted.

1.01 is the `bayesnec` default throughout: `rhat()`, `summary()` and
`check_sampling()` agree on it. Which cutoff was used is still a decision to
record rather than a default to accept silently, since it determines what is
excluded in step 5.

Inspect chain mixing for anything marginal, rather than for everything:

```{r chains, eval = FALSE}
worst <- check_sampling(fit)
check_chains(pull_out(fit, model = worst$model[which.max(worst$max_rhat)]))
```

# 4. Fit diagnostics

Rhat says the sampler explored the posterior. It says nothing about whether the
model *fits*. Two checks, and they answer different questions.

`pp_check()` asks whether data simulated from the fitted model resemble the data
observed, overall:

```{r ppcheck}
pp_check(pull_out(fit, model = "nec4param"))
```

`check_fit()` asks the sharper question: whether the model reproduces the
observed **variability** and the observed **level** *locally*, within each
concentration group, with the control flagged.

For a `bayesmanecfit` it returns every group of every candidate model, which is
more than can be read at once. Two views are useful. The first is the control
row of each model, which is the row that matters most, since `nsec()` reads its
reference from the control:

```{r checkfit-control}
cf <- check_fit(fit)
cf[cf$control, ]
```

The second is the full table for a single model, read when one candidate is in
question:

```{r checkfit-one}
check_fit(pull_out(fit, model = "nec4param"))
```

The non-finite entries in `sd_ratio` are a property of the response rather than
a fault in the fit. A binomial group in which every animal survived, or none
did, has no within-group variability, so the observed residual standard
deviation is exactly zero: the ratio is `Inf` where the model simulates some
spread and the data show none, `NaN` where neither does, and 0 where the reverse
holds. Read `obs_sd` and `sim_sd` directly wherever that occurs. It is also the
reason the continuous response below is the better illustration of
`check_fit()`.

```{r checkfit-plot}
plot(check_fit(pull_out(fit, model = "nec4param")))
```

The plot carries what the table does not, which is the magnitude and the
direction of any discrepancy. Each panel shows the observed statistic
against the 95% span of what the model simulates; a point outside its bar is a
group the model does not reproduce. Location and scale are separate panels
because they fail independently. A model can reproduce every group's mean while
simulating far too much spread, which is the case this check exists to catch.

This is local by necessity. A global dispersion statistic averages over the
curve and can report no discrepancy while the model simulates far more
variability than the data show in the control region, which is exactly the
region *NSEC* is read off. The per-group table is what makes that visible.

Read the posterior predictive p-value as a screen rather than as a test: values
near 0 or 1 mean the model is not reproducing that group, and the control row is
the one to read first.

# 5. Exclusion and its effect on the estimates

This is the step with no coverage elsewhere.

```{r before}
weights_before <- fit$mod_stats[, c("model", "wi")]
weights_before[order(-weights_before$wi), ]
nec_before <- nec(fit)
nec_before
```

Screen on the diagnostics from step 3, then drop:

```{r exclude, message = TRUE}
fit_screened <- screen_models(fit)
```

`screen_models()` runs the same diagnostics, drops what failed, and **says what
went and why**: one line per model, naming every threshold it missed rather than
only the first. That message is not decoration. It is what a methods section has
to cite, and an exclusion step that leaves no record is not reproducible.

The `dispersion` messages in that output follow from the response rather than
from the screen. Several groups sit at complete survival or complete mortality,
where the binomial variance $np(1-p)$ is numerically zero and the Pearson
residual has no denominator. `dispersion()` declines to report rather than
returning a value computed from zero, and the fit itself is unaffected.

It screens on the **sampler only**. A poor `check_fit()` is a modelling result
rather than a reason the fit is unusable, and dropping on it silently would hide
exactly what step 4 exists to show. The two are reported side by side and only
one of them is acted on.

If every candidate fails, `screen_models()` stops with an error listing the
reasons rather than returning a meaningless average. That is the right outcome,
and a signal to return to step 2 rather than to loosen a threshold. If none
fails, it says so and returns the set unchanged.

```{r after}
weights_after <- fit_screened$mod_stats[, c("model", "wi")]
weights_after[order(-weights_after$wi), ]
nec_after <- nec(fit_screened)
rbind(before = nec_before, after = nec_after)
```

```{r shift}
dropped <- setdiff(weights_before$model, weights_after$model)
dropped_wi <- sum(weights_before$wi[weights_before$model %in% dropped])
shift <- (nec_after[["Q50"]] - nec_before[["Q50"]]) / nec_before[["Q50"]]
c(models_dropped = length(dropped), weight_dropped = round(dropped_wi, 3),
  median_shift = round(shift, 4))
```

Two consequences follow, and they are commonly conflated.

**Dropping a model does not rescale the weights of the models that remain.**
`amend()` recomputes them over the reduced set. `bayesnec` weights by pseudo-BMA
with the Bayesian bootstrap --- `loo_model_weights(method = "pseudobma")`, whose
`BB` argument defaults to `TRUE` --- which resamples the pointwise expected log
predictive density. The recomputed weights are therefore not in general the
retained ones divided by the weight they held between them:

```{r rescale-check}
keep <- match(weights_after$model, weights_before$model)
rescaled <- weights_before$wi[keep] / sum(weights_before$wi[keep])
data.frame(model = weights_after$model,
           rescaled = round(rescaled, 3),
           recomputed = round(weights_after$wi, 3))
```

The two columns would be identical if dropping a model renormalised what was
left. They are not, and the ordering of the survivors can change as well as
their magnitude, so the weights of a screened set have to be read from the
screened fit.

**Weight redistribution and movement of the estimate are separate.** The
excluded models carried `r round(100 * dropped_wi)`% of the weight here, and the
model-averaged *N(S)EC* moved by `r sprintf("%.1f", 100 * shift)`%. An excluded
model shifts the estimate only to the extent that its predictions differed from
those of the models retained: removing weight is not the same as removing
information.

That is a property of this dataset rather than a general rule. The two further
cases below give the other outcomes: one where the estimate does move, and one
where nothing fails at all. What a methods section reports is the screen that
was carried out and what it removed, not a consequence assumed in advance.

```{r plot-after}
autoplot(fit_screened)
```

# 6. Reporting

What to state in a methods section:

- **the family, and how it was chosen** --- including the dispersion screen and
  its interval, since a reader cannot otherwise tell whether the family was
  decided from the data or defaulted to;
- **the candidate set as fitted**: the set requested, the equations excluded as
  invalid for the family, and any model that failed to fit at all;
- **the diagnostic thresholds used** --- all three, since they are choices and
  they determine what is excluded. `check_sampling()`'s defaults are 1.01, 400
  and 10; say if you moved any of them and why;
- **which models were excluded and why**, with the weights before and after, so
  that a reader can see what the screen did rather than take it on trust;
- **the model-averaged estimate with its credible interval**, and the weights of
  the retained models.

```{r report}
summary(fit_screened)
```

# A continuous response

The screen in step 1 and the checks in step 4 behave differently for a
continuous response. The `herbicide` data record photosynthetic yield, a
proportion bounded by 0 and 1 but not a count, which is the `beta` case.

`simazine` is chosen for a reason that applies to any Beta response. A `Beta`
response is supported on the open interval (0, 1), so a value recorded as
exactly 0 or 1 lies outside the family's support. `bayesnec` substitutes one
tenth of the smallest non-zero value for a zero, which keeps the fit possible,
but a series that spends several concentrations at the boundary is then being
modelled through that substitution rather than as recorded. `simazine` spans
0.16--0.71 and reaches neither boundary. Several of the other herbicides in this
dataset do: `irgarol` records ten zeros, eight of its twelve replicates at the
highest concentration. Read the response before choosing the family rather than
after; and where zeros are part of the process rather than a recording floor,
see `vignette("example6")`, which covers the families that model them
explicitly.

```{r herbicide, results = "hide"}
data(herbicide)
simazine <- herbicide[herbicide$herbicide == "simazine", ]
fit_b <- bnec(fvfm ~ crf(log(concentration), model = "decline"),
              data = simazine, family = Beta(link = "identity"),
              iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

Two differences follow.

- **There is no family choice to make.** `beta` already carries a free
  dispersion parameter (`phi`), so the over-dispersion screen has nothing to
  decide. The screen in step 1 is specifically a `binomial` and `poisson`
  question.
- **`check_fit()` is more informative here**, because a beta response has
  genuine residual variability at every concentration, where a binomial response
  near total mortality has almost none and every group looks well fitted by
  construction.

```{r herbicide-check}
cf_b <- check_fit(fit_b)
cf_b[cf_b$control, ]
```

The exclusion step runs exactly as before.

```{r herbicide-screen, message = TRUE}
fit_b_screened <- screen_models(fit_b)
```

```{r herbicide-shift}
wb_before <- fit_b$mod_stats[, c("model", "wi")]
wb_after <- fit_b_screened$mod_stats[, c("model", "wi")]
dropped_b <- setdiff(wb_before$model, wb_after$model)
dropped_wi_b <- sum(wb_before$wi[wb_before$model %in% dropped_b])
nec_b <- rbind(before = nec(fit_b), after = nec(fit_b_screened))
nec_b
c(models_dropped = length(dropped_b), weight_dropped = round(dropped_wi_b, 3))
```

Here the excluded models carry `r round(100 * dropped_wi_b)`% of the weight,
less than in the `nassarius` case, and the estimate moves where in that case it
did not. The same screen at the same thresholds produced a different consequence
on a different dataset, which is the reason it has to be run rather than
reasoned about.

# A set with nothing to exclude

`diuron` gives the third outcome, and the one a well-behaved analysis should
expect most often.

```{r diuron, results = "hide"}
diuron <- herbicide[herbicide$herbicide == "diuron", ]
fit_c <- bnec(fvfm ~ crf(log(concentration), model = "decline"),
              data = diuron, family = Beta(link = "identity"),
              iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

```{r diuron-screen, message = TRUE}
check_sampling(fit_c)
fit_c_screened <- screen_models(fit_c)
```

A clean set is itself a result to report. The methods statement is that the
screen was applied at stated thresholds and removed nothing, which is a stronger
claim than silence about whether it was applied at all.

The rest of the workflow --- steps 4 and 6 --- is identical for all three.

